   "bottlenecks": [
    "applications",
    "deployment_platforms",
    "evaluations"
   ],
   "case": "Use is spreading, but slowly and unevenly. The Census Bureau's survey of American businesses finds [fact:firms_using_ai] of firms using AI in their work. This site sets the normal range for that reading at the pace at which personal computers and online selling spread through firms, and the reading sits inside it. Ramp, a company that handles businesses' card spending and whose customers lean towards technology, puts the median (the middle firm's figure when all are ranked) AI spending per employee at [fact:ramp_spend], against [fact:ramp_spend_year_ago] a year before. Payoffs are hard to find so far: in a survey of executives published in early 2026, most said AI had made no measurable difference to their own firm, and in another survey of large firms most AI pilots (trial projects) built into their systems showed no measurable effect on profit. Studies of single jobs find real but modest gains, as in one study of customer-support staff using an AI assistant. Many workers in the jobs most exposed to AI (those whose tasks AI can most readily do, on researchers' measures) do not yet use it weekly, on the research this site follows. This site's automatability census asks, trade by trade, whether the owner would keep a saving, and finds the answer turns on how the trade sends its bills: by the hour, and the client keeps it; by a fee set elsewhere, and the firm can.",
   "commoditising": "This site judges that AI itself is becoming a commodity to the adopting firm: the same tools are sold to it and to its rivals, and their price keeps falling. David Teece, an economist who studied why the firm that invents something often fails to keep the returns, found that generic inputs anyone can buy earn only an ordinary return; on his account a firm's advantage from AI lasts only as long as its rivals are slower to adopt it.",
   "converts_if": "Early adopters rebuild their operations around AI in ways rivals take years to copy, process power in Helmer's sense; or they hold prices that are set by something other than their own costs, such as a share of what the client spends or a fee a regulator sets, so the saving stays with them. Ronald Coase, an economist who studied why some work is done inside firms rather than bought, would add that cheaper checking and contracting could also move work out of firms to AI-run suppliers, handing the saving to whoever runs them.",
   "direction": "commoditising",
   "id": "vc_adopters",
   "kill_shot": "Firms that adopt AI earning higher profits than rivals that do not for years after the tools became widely available, in trades where prices are set by competition; or the share of firms reporting a measurable payoff jumping as use spreads.",
   "layer": "adopters",
   "mechanism": "The adopters are the ordinary firms that buy and use AI, from banks and hospitals to shops and law practices. When AI cuts a firm's costs, the saving can go three ways: to the firm as profit, to its customers as lower prices, or to its workers as higher pay. David Ricardo, an English economist who asked in 1817 why landlords grew rich when bread grew dear, found that the owner of the scarcest input collects a rent: profit above what it takes to keep a business going, earned because something holds rivals off. William Nordhaus, an economist who measured how much of the value of new inventions their makers keep as profit, found that it is a small part and that competition passes most of it on as lower prices. This site judges that the same holds one step down: a firm that adopts AI keeps the saving only until its rivals adopt too, unless its prices are set by something other than its costs. For a layer of buyers, the direction word describes the AI they buy: commoditising means that input is getting cheaper and more alike from seller to seller, so its gains pass to the adopters, unless competition among them passes the gains on again to their own customers.",
   "powers": [],
   "rival": {
    "id": "invisible",
    "title": "Adoption is not slow; it is hard to see"
   },
   "stays_scarce": "This site judges that what stays scarce, for the adopter, is the skill to rebuild its work around AI rather than add AI to old work, and its own records of how that work is done. Hamilton Helmer, a strategy consultant who studied why some firms keep earning more than their investors could expect elsewhere, found that a lasting advantage is a benefit behind a barrier; process power, a way of working that takes rivals years to learn, is the one that could fit an early adopter, but this site cannot yet see it in any reading.",
   "sublayer": null,
   "title": "Firms that adopt AI keep its saving only where competition and their pricing let them; so far use is spreading at the pace of past technologies and few report a payoff"
  }
 ],
